WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.700
<v Instructor>So now let's talk about Aurora Serverless.</v>

2
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.220
It's a serverless offering of the Aurora database.

3
00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:06.960
That means that there is autoscaling

4
00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:09.450
and automated database instantiation

5
00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:11.220
based on your actual usage.

6
00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:14.160
So it's amazing if you have infrequent, intermittent,

7
00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:15.930
or unpredictable workloads.

8
00:00:15.930 --> 00:00:18.270
And there is no need for capacity planning.

9
00:00:18.270 --> 00:00:21.270
You pay per second, and that can be more cost-effective.

10
00:00:21.270 --> 00:00:24.120
So you have the same shared storage volume,

11
00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:27.420
and your client just access a proxy fleet managed by Aurora;

12
00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:30.030
and automatically, based on your usage,

13
00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:32.250
the Aurora is going to scale in the backend

14
00:00:32.250 --> 00:00:34.110
for your serverless database.

15
00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:35.700
So from a client perspective,

16
00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:37.350
you just connect to one endpoint,

17
00:00:37.350 --> 00:00:40.740
and the rest is happening sort of magically in the backend,

18
00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:41.760
which is great, again,

19
00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:44.340
if you don't know how you want to scale,

20
00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:45.930
if you want to have zero usage

21
00:00:45.930 --> 00:00:48.960
or then 1,000 requests per second the second after:

22
00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:51.930
this can be possible with Aurora Serverless.

23
00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:52.830
On top of it,

24
00:00:52.830 --> 00:00:55.110
Aurora Serverless has the Data API,

25
00:00:55.110 --> 00:00:57.870
which allows you to access the Aurora Serverless database

26
00:00:57.870 --> 00:01:01.080
without establishing a JDBC connection.

27
00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:04.260
The only thing you have is a secure HTTPS endpoint

28
00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:06.450
that allows you to run SQL statements.

29
00:01:06.450 --> 00:01:09.450
That means that your application does not have to have

30
00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:12.660
a complicated persistent database connection managements

31
00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:13.560
and so on.

32
00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:15.630
So there is just one API to access,

33
00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:17.610
and then the API is going to be authorized

34
00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:20.130
if the underlying application has the right

35
00:01:20.130 --> 00:01:22.230
to access Secrets Manager

36
00:01:22.230 --> 00:01:24.570
where the credentials are actually checked.

37
00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:25.530
So how does that work?

38
00:01:25.530 --> 00:01:27.450
Well, your Aurora Serverless database

39
00:01:27.450 --> 00:01:29.160
has a Data API enabled,

40
00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:31.740
and then your application does HTTP API calls,

41
00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:34.380
passing in SQL statements through this Data API

42
00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:37.290
but without providing database credentials.

43
00:01:37.290 --> 00:01:39.510
And then the API itself is going to check

44
00:01:39.510 --> 00:01:42.990
whether or not the application has enough permissions

45
00:01:42.990 --> 00:01:45.450
to access the underlying credentials

46
00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:48.030
of the database in Secrets Manager.

47
00:01:48.030 --> 00:01:50.220
So there's a transparent allow or deny.

48
00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:51.990
And your application, of course,

49
00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:53.370
in its IAM policy,

50
00:01:53.370 --> 00:01:56.160
must have access to Aurora Serverless, the Data API,

51
00:01:56.160 --> 00:01:59.190
but also have access to the underlying secrets

52
00:01:59.190 --> 00:02:00.753
in Secrets Manager.

53
00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:04.620
Alternatively, if you do want to manage connections,

54
00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:06.720
you have RDS Proxy for Aurora.

55
00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:09.600
So an Aurora primary instance, as you know,

56
00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:11.220
can do read and writes.

57
00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:13.950
And so it's possible for you to create an RDS proxy

58
00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:16.050
in front of that primary instance,

59
00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:18.840
and then have a traditional application architecture;

60
00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:20.670
for example, the users talk

61
00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:22.350
to the application load balancer,

62
00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:23.820
which talks to the Lambda function,

63
00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:25.920
and then the RDS proxy.

64
00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:28.530
But on top of it, RDS proxy for Aurora

65
00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:32.460
has the ability to create a RDS proxy

66
00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:35.250
only for the read-only endpoints of Aurora.

67
00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:37.350
So only for the Read Replicas.

68
00:02:37.350 --> 00:02:39.330
So that means that you have your primary instance

69
00:02:39.330 --> 00:02:40.950
that does read and writes, of course,

70
00:02:40.950 --> 00:02:43.830
but also Read Replicas 1 and 2 and so on.

71
00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:45.720
And you can create an RDS proxy,

72
00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:49.440
which is a read-only endpoint for your read-only replicas.

73
00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:50.880
And therefore your Lambda functions

74
00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:52.260
will have a read-only endpoint

75
00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:55.230
that is having the same feature of RDS Proxy

76
00:02:55.230 --> 00:02:57.363
but for your Read Replicas.

77
00:02:58.590 --> 00:02:59.430
You also have the option

78
00:02:59.430 --> 00:03:01.470
to create a global database in Aurora.

79
00:03:01.470 --> 00:03:04.410
So if you create cross-region replications,

80
00:03:04.410 --> 00:03:06.540
then it's useful for disaster recovery,

81
00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:07.650
and it's simple to put in place.

82
00:03:07.650 --> 00:03:09.150
But then you can actually use

83
00:03:09.150 --> 00:03:11.220
the Aurora Global Database feature,

84
00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:12.660
which is the recommended way,

85
00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:14.850
in which you define a primary region,

86
00:03:14.850 --> 00:03:16.650
which takes reads and writes,

87
00:03:16.650 --> 00:03:20.610
and then up to 10 read-only secondary regions.

88
00:03:20.610 --> 00:03:22.920
And these are going to be replicated

89
00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:24.810
from the primary region.

90
00:03:24.810 --> 00:03:27.210
And the guarantee is that the replication lag

91
00:03:27.210 --> 00:03:28.740
is less than one second,

92
00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:30.630
which is really good performance.

93
00:03:30.630 --> 00:03:33.870
So you can get up to then 16 Read Replicas

94
00:03:33.870 --> 00:03:35.460
per secondary region.

95
00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:37.320
So it really allows you to scale.

96
00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:39.510
And the reason you would use a global database

97
00:03:39.510 --> 00:03:42.900
would be to be decreasing the latency.

98
00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:46.200
On top of it if the primary region goes down,

99
00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:49.020
then promoting another region as an RTO

100
00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:50.550
of less than one minute.

101
00:03:50.550 --> 00:03:52.770
So it's really, really, really helpful.

102
00:03:52.770 --> 00:03:54.990
And on top of it, there's one more setting

103
00:03:54.990 --> 00:03:56.640
for disaster recovery,

104
00:03:56.640 --> 00:03:59.640
is the ability to manage the RPO in Aurora

105
00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:01.740
for PostgreSQL through a setting,

106
00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:06.360
therefore guaranteeing that you have less lag and so on.

107
00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:07.530
So let's have a look.

108
00:04:07.530 --> 00:04:09.570
We have applications that read and write

109
00:04:09.570 --> 00:04:11.940
to your Aurora database in its primary region,

110
00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:13.560
and then you have a secondary region.

111
00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:14.550
You have some replication.

112
00:04:14.550 --> 00:04:17.430
You can set up again an RPO setting

113
00:04:17.430 --> 00:04:20.850
for PostgreSQL version of Aurora,

114
00:04:20.850 --> 00:04:24.450
and then other regions can have read-only applications.

115
00:04:24.450 --> 00:04:25.440
But for Global Aurora,

116
00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:27.150
there's also another feature you need to know about

117
00:04:27.150 --> 00:04:29.040
called write forwarding.

118
00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:32.970
So this enables actually your secondary database clusters

119
00:04:32.970 --> 00:04:34.200
to forward SQL statements

120
00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:36.150
that try to perform a write operation

121
00:04:36.150 --> 00:04:38.190
on the primary database cluster.

122
00:04:38.190 --> 00:04:41.340
So even though only the primary database cluster

123
00:04:41.340 --> 00:04:42.780
is accepting writes,

124
00:04:42.780 --> 00:04:45.120
your users can try submitting writes

125
00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:49.080
to a secondary region for your Aurora Global Database.

126
00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:51.180
And then this is going to be forwarded

127
00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:53.520
to the primary database cluster.

128
00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:55.380
So of course, database,

129
00:04:55.380 --> 00:04:57.660
the data is always going to be changed first

130
00:04:57.660 --> 00:04:59.610
on the primary database cluster.

131
00:04:59.610 --> 00:05:03.090
And then through the replication mechanism already in place,

132
00:05:03.090 --> 00:05:04.710
it is going to be replicated

133
00:05:04.710 --> 00:05:06.420
to the secondary database cluster.

134
00:05:06.420 --> 00:05:08.370
So the writes actually never happen

135
00:05:08.370 --> 00:05:11.430
directly on the secondary database cluster.

136
00:05:11.430 --> 00:05:13.920
Therefore, the primary database cluster

137
00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:17.970
is the one with the always up-to-date copy of all data.

138
00:05:17.970 --> 00:05:19.500
So why would you do a write forwarding

139
00:05:19.500 --> 00:05:22.830
and not just write straight to the primary database cluster?

140
00:05:22.830 --> 00:05:24.270
Well, this has to do with reducing

141
00:05:24.270 --> 00:05:25.890
the number of endpoints to manage,

142
00:05:25.890 --> 00:05:28.800
because you can tell your applications in other regions

143
00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:31.530
to just connect to that secondary region,

144
00:05:31.530 --> 00:05:34.110
and they can do read and writes just as they want;

145
00:05:34.110 --> 00:05:35.220
and at some point,

146
00:05:35.220 --> 00:05:38.223
the write will be forwarded and replicated back, okay?

147
00:05:39.090 --> 00:05:40.380
You also need to know

148
00:05:40.380 --> 00:05:43.440
how to convert an RDS database into an Aurora database.

149
00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:46.020
So the RDS database instance, there are two ways:

150
00:05:46.020 --> 00:05:48.570
you can first make a database snapshot,

151
00:05:48.570 --> 00:05:49.560
and then from that snapshot,

152
00:05:49.560 --> 00:05:50.700
you can straight restore it

153
00:05:50.700 --> 00:05:52.890
into an Aurora database instance.

154
00:05:52.890 --> 00:05:54.870
So this is good, but in case your applications

155
00:05:54.870 --> 00:05:57.600
are still using the main RDS database instance,

156
00:05:57.600 --> 00:05:59.670
you're going to have some problem

157
00:05:59.670 --> 00:06:02.250
with the replication of the remaining data,

158
00:06:02.250 --> 00:06:04.530
or you would have to take the first database down,

159
00:06:04.530 --> 00:06:06.720
then take a snapshot, then restore it.

160
00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:08.940
But this has the benefits of being quite quick,

161
00:06:08.940 --> 00:06:11.280
the snapshot and restore feature.

162
00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:12.450
Now, the other option

163
00:06:12.450 --> 00:06:15.030
is to create an actual Aurora Read Replica

164
00:06:15.030 --> 00:06:16.740
on your RDS database instance.

165
00:06:16.740 --> 00:06:18.150
So it is completely possible

166
00:06:18.150 --> 00:06:19.980
to create an Aurora Read Replica

167
00:06:19.980 --> 00:06:22.230
on an RDS database instance.

168
00:06:22.230 --> 00:06:25.320
And this Read Replica is going to be replicated.

169
00:06:25.320 --> 00:06:28.110
And when the replication lag is close to zero,

170
00:06:28.110 --> 00:06:31.350
you can promote it to its own Aurora database instance

171
00:06:31.350 --> 00:06:35.310
and migrate your application from RDS to Aurora.

172
00:06:35.310 --> 00:06:36.570
So that's it for this lecture.

173
00:06:36.570 --> 00:06:39.570
I hope you liked it, and I will see you in the next lecture.

